topics.push({
  id:'t12-008',title:'system design tradeoffs and decision methods',
  concept:'A tradeoff is a consequence of meeting one requirement under another constraint, not a slogan or a universal ranking. State the invariant and workload, compare mechanisms at the same abstraction level, then name operational and failure costs.',
  overview:'System design interviews and production reviews often revisit the same decision families: data models, consistency, replication, scaling, caching, interfaces, and event delivery. The correct answer depends on workload and consequences. Terms such as NoSQL, strong consistency, microservices, and exactly once are too broad to justify a design unless translated into specific contracts.',
  walkthrough:'For a ticket reservation, first state the invariant that one seat cannot be sold twice. A strongly coordinated inventory write may be appropriate even if browsing uses a stale cache. A payment workflow spanning an external processor cannot share one ordinary database transaction; model reservation, payment outcome, timeout, compensation, and reconciliation. This combines several tradeoffs without pretending one architecture label resolves them.',
  refs:tradeoffReferences,
  practice:'Choose a storage, consistency, deployment, API, and event-delivery approach for a globally used inventory service. For each decision, write the invariant, workload assumption, alternative, failure behavior, and operational cost.',
  solution:'Keep the invariant explicit: inventory cannot be oversold. Compare a single-region transactional baseline with multi-region options using measured write latency, recovery objectives, and conflict semantics. Use a query/API contract suited to clients and cacheability, while documenting schema evolution. Choose event delivery based on whether consumers need task dispatch or retained replay. Make cache freshness bounded and never use it as the final allocation authority. Revisit each decision if assumptions change.',
  interview:'For every proposed mechanism, answer: what requirement does it meet; what is the simpler alternative; what guarantee is actually provided; what fails during timeout, partition, retry, or recovery; how is it operated; and what evidence would change the decision? Avoid false dichotomies: hybrids are valid when boundaries and guarantees are clear.',
  compares:tradeoffCatalog.groups.map(g=>g.items.map(x=>x.name)),sourceKeys:[],sourceUrls:[handbook+'trade-offs/','https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110','https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/mvcc.html','https://sre.google/sre-book/table-of-contents/','https://groups.csail.mit.edu/tds/papers/Gilbert/Brewer2.pdf','https://www.cs.umd.edu/~abadi/papers/abadi-pacelc.pdf']
});
