fs.writeFileSync('curriculum/system-design-case-studies.md',`# System-design case-study index\n\nThe HLD Handbook lists 56 system-design cases across consumer products, infrastructure, financial systems, AI, and developer platforms. This index links to an independently authored practice lesson for every listed case. The source topic list is adapted from [The HLD Handbook](https://hld.handbook.academy/curriculum/case-studies/) (CC BY-SA 4.0); this adapted index is shared under CC BY-SA 4.0. The explanations and answer guides in this project are original course writing.\n\n| # | Case | Lesson |\n|---:|---|---|\n${caseStudies.map((c,i)=>`| ${i+1} | ${c.name} | [Open lesson](../course/drafts/t12-006/t12-006-practice-${c.id}.md) |`).join('\n')}\n`);
for (const t of topics) {
  const topicSources=t.sourceUrls??sources(...t.sourceKeys);
  const manifest = {
    schemaVersion:4,id:t.id,track:'track-12',title:t.title,status:'source-backed-draft-generation-enabled',
    concepts:[t.concept, 'Translate product requirements into observable measures and testable user journeys before choosing components.', 'Make state ownership, consistency, replay, and recovery explicit at every boundary.', 'Account for latency, throughput, tail behavior, capacity, cost, security, privacy, and operational effort as interacting constraints.', 'Use a simple baseline, then add a mechanism only to meet a stated requirement or measured bottleneck.', 'Design failure behavior and release compatibility across client, API, data, and operations rather than per component.'],
    inventory:t.refs.map(g=>({id:g.id,title:g.title,items:Object.keys(g.entries)})), inventoryComplete:true,
    comparisons:t.compares,comparisonSources:topicSources,versionNotes:[],
    sourceSnapshot:{authority:'The HLD Handbook public system-design syllabus, with relevant standards, framework guidance, and official platform documentation for technical context.',edition:'Handbook syllabus checked 2026-11-17; concepts are version-independent unless an implementation-specific source says otherwise.',checkedAt:'2026-11-17',urls:topicSources},
    scopeExclusions:t.id==='t12-007'?['The 67 product names are a snapshot of the Handbook technology index, not all products in the market. Product roles are orientation only; verify exact current capabilities, limits, versions, pricing, and regional availability in primary vendor documentation.']:t.id==='t12-008'?['The 22 tradeoff guides are the named scope from the Handbook index; each production decision needs workload-specific evidence and a protocol/product-specific source.']:['Handbook module titles are mapped in curriculum/system-design-handbook-module-coverage.json; title mapping does not imply full depth or completed factual review.','Vendor-specific service SKUs, quotas, and configuration signatures are intentionally excluded; verify those in the selected provider’s current documentation.','The inventory names are the closed reference scope for this topic; the overview teaches the mental model without listing every item.'],generationAllowed:true,
  };
  if(t.id==='t12-006') manifest.practiceProblems=caseStudies.map(c=>({id:c.id,name:c.name,difficulty:'advanced',patterns:c.focus.split(', '),statement:c.statement,constraints:['Define requirements, invariant, workload, and source of truth before selecting components.','Cover peak demand, partial failure, recovery, security, and operational evidence.'],examples:[{input:'The interviewer changes one workload or failure assumption during the discussion.',output:'Update the affected path and explain which decision changes and why.'}],sourceUrls:[handbook,official.googleSre,official.azureArch,official.azureWell]}));
  const dir=`curriculum/manifests/system-design`; fs.mkdirSync(dir,{recursive:true});
  fs.writeFileSync(`${dir}/${t.id}.json`,JSON.stringify(manifest,null,2)+'\n');
  const draftDir=`course/drafts/${t.id}`;fs.mkdirSync(draftDir,{recursive:true});
  const cite=topicSources.map((u,i)=>`[${i===0?'HLD Handbook':'reference '+i}](${u})`).join(', ');
  const put=(suffix,title,body)=>fs.writeFileSync(`${draftDir}/${t.id}-${suffix}.md`,`# ${t.title} — ${title}\n\nSources: ${cite}.\n\n${body.trim()}\n`);
  put('overview','Big-picture orientation and mental model',`## Problem and purpose\n\n${t.overview}\n\n## Plain-language mental model\n\nFirst understand the promise to the user. Then trace data and control from the user to the authoritative state and back. Treat every network hop as a possible delay or failure. Separate synchronous work needed for the response from durable asynchronous work that can finish later.\n\n## How the parts fit together\n\n\`\`\`text\nuser goal → requirements + workload → simplest correct end-to-end path\n          → ownership + contracts → capacity, consistency, and security\n          → failure behavior + observability → measured iteration\n\`\`\`\n\n## Concept map\n\nThe major choices fall into five connected questions: where requests enter and how they are routed; where authoritative and derived data live; how work is coordinated across processes; which guarantees users observe during delay or failure; and how the service is operated, secured, and evolved. A component is useful only when it answers one of those questions.\n\n## End-to-end walkthrough\n\n${t.walkthrough}\n\n## When to use it and tradeoffs\n\nUse these concepts when defining a new service, reviewing a scaling proposal, investigating an incident, or preparing an architecture interview. Prefer the smallest design that meets measured goals. Distribution improves some forms of scale and isolation but adds network failure, partial success, consistency choices, and on-call burden.\n\n## What later lessons cover\n\nThe concepts lesson develops the decision process and failure reasoning. Reference lessons explain each named item in bounded groups. Practice and interview lessons apply the ideas to realistic scenarios. Existing .NET, React, SQL, security, and operations tracks remain the place for framework-specific APIs and configuration.`);
  put('concepts','Core concepts and how they fit together',`## Learning objectives\n\nAfter this lesson, you should be able to turn a product goal into constraints, model load, identify authoritative state, choose an appropriate consistency boundary, reason about partial failure, and defend a simple design with evidence.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\nKnow HTTP request/response, relational transactions, async work, authentication versus authorization, and basic deployment. The existing programming, browser, database, security, and operations stages supply these foundations.\n\n## Begin with the practical picture\n\n${t.overview}\n\nBefore memorizing the technical names, restate this problem as a user story. Identify what the user expects, what can go wrong, and what information must remain correct. Sketch the simplest successful path and one failure path in plain language. Then use the following concepts and reference lessons to choose mechanisms that meet those needs. The technical terms describe tools and guarantees; they do not replace understanding the problem.\n\n## Core concepts\n\n### Requirements are executable design inputs\n\nSeparate functional behavior from quality attributes. Ask who uses the system, what must happen, what must never happen, and how success is measured. “Fast” needs an operation, boundary, percentile, workload, and time window. “Reliable” needs a good-event definition and recovery behavior. Rank requirements so the design has a reason to make tradeoffs.\n\n### Workload reveals the shape of the problem\n\nEstimate average and peak arrival rate, burst duration, concurrency, payload size, read/write ratio, fan-out, data growth, and retention. Use ranges when evidence is weak. A useful estimate exposes a bottleneck or rules out an option; false precision does neither. Validate with representative load and compare estimates with production.\n\n### Correctness precedes optimization\n\nName the source of truth and transaction boundary. Decide which operations require atomicity, which reads may be stale, what ordering is required, and what duplicate work means. Then distinguish serving replicas and derived indexes from authoritative data. Every asynchronous path needs acknowledgement, retry, deduplication, and recovery rules.\n\n### Networks create partial failure\n\nA timeout means the caller did not learn the outcome; it does not prove that the remote operation failed. Calls need deadlines, bounded concurrency, cancellation, and selective retries. Retrying a non-idempotent operation can duplicate effects. A circuit breaker can limit calls to an unhealthy dependency, but cannot make that dependency healthy.\n\n### Scaling is a capacity and ownership question\n\nVertical scaling increases resources for a node; horizontal scaling adds nodes and requires routing, shared or partitioned state, and failure-aware capacity. Caches reduce repeated work but introduce freshness; queues smooth bursts but can accumulate delay; sharding increases write capacity but creates key-selection and hot-partition concerns. Measure the constrained resource first.\n\n### Reliability is behavior under defined faults\n\nState what users see when a dependency, zone, region, or provider fails. Define error budgets and recovery targets, bound queues and retries, isolate optional work, and exercise restoration. Redundancy is meaningful only if remaining capacity, independent failure domains, and failover procedures meet the target.\n\n## Mechanisms and relationships\n\nA typical request crosses a browser or client, an edge or load-balancing layer, an application boundary, data stores, and possibly a broker or external provider. At each boundary ask: who authenticates and authorizes; what data crosses; what timeout/deadline applies; what is recorded; and whether a retry is safe. Distributed traces connect spans across services, while aggregate metrics reveal saturation and user-level error rates.\n\nIn .NET, propagate cancellation tokens through ASP.NET Core handlers, HttpClient, and database calls; use bounded worker concurrency and explicit transaction boundaries. EF Core transactions protect database work but do not atomically publish to an unrelated broker, which is why an outbox is useful. In React, the UI must represent loading, stale, empty, error, and retry states honestly; client cancellation cannot revoke a server-side side effect. SSR, hydration, caching, and API deployments must remain compatible during rolling releases.\n\n## Worked examples\n\nFor a read-heavy catalog, begin with database indexes and query shape, measure cacheable traffic, then introduce a CDN or application cache with an explicit key and freshness policy. For a payment, prefer a durable idempotent command and ledger-like state transitions over blind retries. For a report, asynchronous processing is often appropriate if acceptance is durable and users can inspect status.\n\n## Misconceptions and edge cases\n\n- A replica is not automatically a backup; replication may copy corruption or deletion.\n- “Exactly once” usually describes a bounded protocol guarantee, not end-to-end business effect across arbitrary services.\n- Autoscaling has reaction delay and cannot compensate for an unbounded queue or exhausted database.\n- Eventual consistency does not promise a particular maximum staleness.\n- Multi-region increases availability only when data placement, conflict rules, routing, and recovery are tested.\n- More services can increase independent deployment, but also increase coordination and operational load.\n\n## Knowledge checks with answers\n\n1. **A request timed out. Is it safe to retry?** Only when the operation is idempotent or the outcome can be verified with a stable operation key; timeout alone leaves the result unknown.\n2. **Why add a cache?** To meet a measured latency/capacity need when a defined freshness and invalidation policy is acceptable.\n3. **What makes a queue durable?** Its configured persistence/replication and acknowledgement contract, plus retention and tested consumer recovery; the word “queue” alone is not a guarantee.\n4. **When split a service?** When independent ownership, scaling, security, or release needs outweigh network and operational costs.`);
  for(const g of t.refs){
    const entries=Object.entries(g.entries).map(([name,explanation])=>`- \`${name}\` — ${explanation}`).join('\n');
    const taskTitle=`Reference: ${g.title}`;
    fs.writeFileSync(`${draftDir}/${t.id}-reference-${g.id}.md`,`# ${t.title} — ${taskTitle}\n\nSources: ${cite}.\n\n## Scope and version\n\nThis is a conceptual reference, not a vendor configuration guide. The supplied list below is the complete named scope for this lesson. Exact service limits and implementation details vary; consult the cited standard or current product documentation before deployment.\n\n## How this group fits the mental model\n\nThese concepts are tools for solving different parts of the system path. Select them from workload, correctness, and failure requirements. Combining tools does not automatically compose their guarantees.\n\n## Supplied names explained\n\n${entries}\n\n## Cross-item comparison\n\nUse the item descriptions above to compare purpose, guarantee, and cost. Choose the smallest mechanism that provides the needed behavior; similar names may operate at different layers or under different assumptions.\n\n## Examples\n\nFor a new feature, trace one successful operation and one failure through the relevant items. Identify the authoritative component, the state that is copied or derived, and what happens when a request is delayed, repeated, or interrupted.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Selecting a component by popularity before defining the requirement.\n- Treating a protocol or product name as a guarantee without reading its contract.\n- Ignoring cost, operational ownership, observability, and recovery.\n- Applying one consistency or caching rule to data with different correctness needs.\n\n## Knowledge checks with answers\n\n1. **Which item is the source of truth?** That is an explicit application data-ownership decision, not implied by a cache or replica.\n2. **What if an acknowledgement is lost?** Treat the outcome as unknown; use idempotency or verification before repeating an effect.\n3. **How do you choose among alternatives?** Compare them against the named workload, guarantee, cost, operational skill, and failure scenario.`);
  }
  if(t.id!=='t12-006') put('practice','Exercises and worked solutions',`## Goals\n\nApply the topic's system model to a concrete workload, explain assumptions, and identify the correctness and operational questions that need answers.\n\n## Worked example\n\n${t.practice}\n\n**Worked solution.** ${t.solution}\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Restate the product goal as two functional requirements and three measurable quality objectives.\n2. Estimate the average, peak, and burst workload. List the assumptions that most affect your estimate.\n3. Identify authoritative data and one derived representation. State how the derived data is refreshed and reconciled.\n4. Walk through a timeout after a write. What does the client know, and what key or query resolves ambiguity?\n5. Remove one dependency from the design. Describe user-visible degradation and the telemetry that should detect it.\n6. Explain which single measurement would justify the next scaling change.\n\n## Progressive hints\n\nStart with user behavior and invariants. Draw a baseline before adding components. Follow the write path and read path separately. Distinguish successful completion from durable acceptance and unknown outcome.\n\n## Complete solutions\n\n1. Functional requirements describe specific user actions and outcomes. Quality targets state boundary, workload, metric, threshold, and measurement window; they should be agreed with the product owner.\n2. Use ranges and show the arithmetic. Include peak-to-average ratio, concurrency, payload, and retention; mark estimates that need measurement.\n3. Name the component that owns the authoritative update. A derived store needs a delivery/rebuild policy and a freshness objective.\n4. A timeout is ambiguous. Retry only with an idempotency key or verify the operation by a stable identifier before issuing a new effect.\n5. Preserve critical behavior and degrade optional work. Emit dependency latency/error, request outcome, queue age where relevant, and trace context without high-cardinality sensitive fields.\n6. Select evidence for the actual constraint, such as database saturation, tail latency, queue age, or cache miss cost. Scale the constrained component and retest.\n\n## Tests and edge cases\n\nTest duplicate requests, slow consumers, a full queue, stale cache, one node or zone unavailable, provider timeout after success, schema change during mixed-version rollout, unauthorized tenant ID, and recovery from backup.\n\n## Reflection questions\n\nWhich assumption could invalidate the design? Which invariant would be hardest to recover after a partial failure? What evidence would make you remove a component rather than add another?`);
  put('interview','Interview questions and reasoning',`## Core questions\n\n1. What user outcome and invariant does the system protect?\n2. What is the workload shape and likely bottleneck?\n3. Which data is authoritative, and what can be stale or rebuilt?\n4. What happens after timeout, duplicate delivery, or partial failure?\n5. How are identity, tenant isolation, privacy, and abuse handled?\n6. Which signals prove the design meets its objectives?\n\n## Concise interview answers\n\n${t.interview}\n\n## Deep follow-up answers\n\nA strong design starts with a concise requirements summary and states assumptions. It estimates only enough to drive architecture, traces one write and read, then examines hot keys, data growth, backpressure, failure domains, and recovery. It names the limits of every mechanism and leaves time to summarize decisions and unresolved risks.\n\n## Scenario questions\n\n- A database commit may have succeeded although the caller timed out. What is your recovery protocol?\n- Traffic grows tenfold but only one tenant drives it. How do you protect other tenants and determine whether partitioning is needed?\n- The queue is healthy by depth but users report delayed work. Which time-based signal and end-to-end trace do you inspect?\n- A deployment changes both API and schema while old instances remain. How do you sequence compatible changes and rollback?\n- Two regions accept conflicting writes. Which operations can merge, and which need a single authority or coordination?\n\n## Tradeoffs and alternatives\n\n${t.compares.map(c=>`- ${c.join(' vs ')}: select based on the workload, correctness guarantee, and operating cost rather than treating either choice as universally superior.`).join('\n')}\n\n## Common weak answers\n\n- “Use microservices, a cache, and a queue” without explaining the requirement each solves.\n- “The request failed because it timed out” when the remote outcome is unknown.\n- “We can scale horizontally” without addressing state, partitioning, or remaining capacity.\n- “The system is available” without defining user-visible success, measurement, or recovery.\n- Drawing components without explaining data ownership or operations.\n\n## Self-check rubric\n\nScore 0–2 for each: requirements and assumptions; workload reasoning; correctness and ownership; read/write path; failure and recovery; security and privacy; observability and validation; cost and tradeoffs; clear communication. A strong answer scores 14–18, states uncertainty, and ties every major component to a requirement.`);
  if(t.id==='t12-006') for(const c of caseStudies){
    const folder=`course/drafts/${t.id}`;const taskTitle=`${t.title} — Problem: ${c.name}`;
    fs.writeFileSync(`${folder}/${t.id}-practice-${c.id}.md`,`# ${taskTitle}\n\nSources: [HLD Handbook](${handbook}), [Google SRE](${official.googleSre}), [Azure architecture guidance](${official.azureArch}).\n\n## Goals\n\nPractice ${c.focus}; state assumptions, draw a correct baseline, and reason through a failure rather than reciting a memorized diagram.\n\n## Worked example\n\n${c.statement}\n\n**Worked design.** ${c.answer}\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Write the top functional requirements and the quality objective that most changes the architecture.\n2. Estimate demand, storage growth, and the likely hot key or constrained resource. State assumptions.\n3. Define authoritative data, identifiers, API behavior, and transaction boundary.\n4. Trace a write and a read, including a timeout or duplicate request.\n5. Describe one scaling change and its consistency, cost, and operational tradeoffs.\n\n## Progressive hints\n\nStart with the invariant and user-visible outcome. Separate write and read paths. Identify a simpler baseline before adding distributed components. Make one failure case concrete.\n\n## Complete solutions\n\n1. Prioritize user-visible correctness and measurable latency, availability, freshness, durability, or cost for the relevant path.\n2. Use ranges for traffic and growth. Identify skew and fan-out rather than assuming uniform keys. Validate the sensitive estimate with a representative benchmark.\n3. Name one authoritative owner for each critical fact. Specify idempotency and compatibility when callers may retry or remain old.\n4. Treat missing acknowledgements as unknown outcomes. Retry only with idempotency or verify state; make asynchronous effects deduplicable.\n5. Add replication, caching, partitioning, or queueing only to meet a stated need; explain the operations burden and recovery plan.\n\n### Case-specific design answer\n\n${c.answer}\n\n## Tests and edge cases\n\n${c.tests}\n\n## Reflection questions\n\nWhich assumption could change your data model? What is the most expensive failure to recover from? What production metric proves the chosen tradeoff is working?\n`);
  }
}

// Remove only obsolete generated Markdown for these authored system-design
